title: Loom Oracle MVP RUNS — Phase 1 engine built, deterministic, read-only on git
summary: 'First real Loom code shipped on branch loom-engine (commit b0725cc8): the Convergence/Divergence Oracle runs end-to-end. @a-company/loom reuses premise-core extraction to ABSORB two git branches into a content-addressed store of MEANING (essence-hash: Merkle over the structured .purpose contract + edges-by-target-essence, never prose/name/path/embeddings; Tarjan SCC for cycles; stable-id disambiguator for generic contracts), diff them (typed SemDeltas; rename = the empty delta), predict the merge (commute/knot/dangle algebra), run git''s actual merge read-only (git merge-tree, never touches HEAD/index/worktree), and score the confusion matrix (agreeClean/agreeConflict + the two falsifiable cells divergeGitOnly ★ and divergeMeaningOnly ★) → .loom/oracle.jsonl. Verified: premise-core 42 + loom 18 tests green; `loom oracle HEAD HEAD` = 0 knots/0 dangling/CONVERGENT, stable across runs; git untouched. THE BUG THE ORACLE CAUGHT ON ITSELF: absorbing a ref twice produced ~26 phantom deltas because the repo has DUPLICATE symbol names across packages (e.g. #yaml-parser defined in BOTH packages/portal/core and packages/purpose/core) whose winner flipped with filesystem order — the known scan-index nondeterminism (T-2026-06-13-011), now caught red-handed by Loom''s own determinism requirement. Fixed Loom-side (deterministic dup-resolution + repo-relative paths); determinism is now a tested invariant (byte-identical stateId + every contentId across two absorbs). NEXT: a fixture branch-pair that exhibits a real divergence (git merges bytes clean but meaning is a knot) — the data that proves ''meaning carries merge-information bytes can''t''.'
